  number={},
  pages={1-18},
  abstract={Supply voltage fluctuations due to large and rapid current consumption in modern high-performance system-on-chips (SoCs), known as supply voltage ( ${V} {_{\text {DD}}}$ ) droops, may incur critical timing violations and functional failures. This article presents a fully digital, reference-less droop detection and mitigation circuit utilizing a shared dual-mode delay line (SDMDL). The proposed circuit unifies a time-to-digital converter (TDC)-based droop detector and a digitally controlled oscillator (DCO)-based droop mitigator into SDMDL, enabling direct ratio-based frequency reduction. Furthermore, a time-domain glitch-free recovery circuit (GFRC) is employed to guarantee seamless clock source switching from DCO to the input clock through phase alignment, preventing timing violations during the recovery phase. Fabricated in a 28-nm CMOS process, the proposed design demonstrates a droop mitigation latency of only half a clock cycle and frequency reduction range of 60% with a fine resolution of 2.5%. In addition, compared to prior art, the design achieves the highest  ${V} {_{\min }}$  reduction of 14.8% and a throughput improvement of 42.9% under a 15%  ${V} {_{\text {DD}}}$  droop while consuming 7.11 mW and occupying 0.0131 mm2.},
