  abstract={Portable audio devices, such as true wireless stereo (TWS) headphones and hearing aids, increasingly rely on digital-input amplifiers to deliver high-quality sound while consuming minimal power. This article presents a digital-input, filter-less Class-D amplifier (CDA) that achieves low noise, low total harmonic distortion plus noise (THD+N), and low idle power simultaneously, alongside optimized small-signal power efficiency. The design employs three key techniques: a power-adaptive three-level main current-steering DAC (M-IDAC), an assistant IDAC (A-IDAC), and a power-efficient dual-edge pulse width adjustment (DE-PWA) modulator. Fabricated in a standard 180-nm CMOS process, the prototype consumes 1.08 mW of idle power from a 1.8-V supply and achieves a 117.8-dB dynamic range (DR). It delivers a peak THD+N of −102.2 dB at a 1-kHz signal and a peak efficiency of 92%.},
