  abstract={This article introduces a pipelined successive-approximation time-to-digital converter (SAR–TDC) that employs architectural and calibration techniques to improve linearity and bit utilization under delay mismatch and PVT variations. The design introduces a new bit-stage structure that enables practical decoupling of dominant error sources, and a noise-shaping SAR–TDC (NS-SAR–TDC) operating mode that facilitates in situ characterization of random delay errors and rapid foreground self-calibration without external hardware. Fabricated in 28-nm CMOS, the prototype achieves 800 MS/s over a ±100 ps input range from a 0.8 V supply, occupying 0.01 mm2, and consuming 1.71 mW. The proposed calibration improves the raw SFDR/SNDR by more than 20/12 dB, corresponding to a 2-bit ENOB enhancement. At Nyquist, the TDC achieves 5.73-bit ENOB with a 3.2 ps LSB, while static integral nonlinearity (INL) remains below 0.23 LSB/0.7 ps, demonstrating robust high-linearity operation and improved bit utilization across multiple chips.},
