  abstract={Future full-duplex (FD) radio systems require highly tunable self-interference (SI) cancellers with rapid adaptation to mitigate dynamic SI profiles in wireless environments. This article presents a 2.4-GHz RF SI canceller chip with extensive tuning capabilities that operates with a machine-learning (ML)-augmented adaptation loop for FD transceivers. The proposed design improves the depth of SI cancellation, canceller linearity, and convergence speed. The prototype canceller chip, fabricated using a 40-nm CMOS process, features enhanced tuning capabilities of the filter coefficients, delay spread, and linearity. A custom-designed neural network (NN) is implemented on an FPGA within a Xilinx RFSoC evaluation board to accelerate the convergence time during the canceller’s adaptation process. The RF canceller, realized as a six-tap complex finite impulse response (FIR) filter with programmable delay spread, achieves 30-dB SI cancellation over a 40-MHz bandwidth in a real wireless channel and 32 dB with a static load, with a post-calibration input-referred third-order intercept point (IIP3) of +38 dBm. The NN computes an initial canceller setting, followed by a dithered linear search (DLS) algorithm, achieving a convergence time of less than 10.5 ms. The SI canceller chip demonstrates a maximum group delay variation of 2 ns, an output noise density of −184 dBV/ $(\text {Hz})^{1/2}$ , and an S11 of −10 dB across 2.3–2.8 GHz. The RF SI canceller chip occupies an active area of approximately 1.8 mm2 and consumes 39 mW.},
