  abstract={Flash-mode direct time-of-flight (dToF) light detection and ranging (LiDAR) is widely adopted in autonomous systems and robotics due to its capability for accurate depth measurement and robustness under challenging lighting conditions. However, scaling single-photon avalanche diode (SPAD)-based dToF pixel arrays toward higher resolution 3-D imaging introduces significant challenges in power consumption and on-chip data handling, particularly when large-volume time-correlated single-photon counting (TCSPC) data are uniformly acquired and processed. This work presents a  $96\times 64$  SPAD-based flash LiDAR sensor employing a dual-mode sensing architecture that combines full-resolution 2-D intensity detection with on-chip edge-guided sparse 3-D depth detection. An on-chip edge extraction circuit (EEC) identifies structurally salient regions from the 2-D intensity map and guides pixel-level skipping during 3-D detection, thereby concentrating time-of-flight (ToF) measurements on informative areas while avoiding unnecessary full-array activation and excessive TCSPC readout. The resulting sparse 3-D depth maps are fused with dense 2-D intensity maps using a multi-modal, AI-assisted reconstruction network, demonstrating that the retained measurements are sufficient to recover full-field depth information. By co-designing on-sensor data acquisition and AI-assisted reconstruction, the proposed architecture reduces the effective 3-D data volume by 87.5% and significantly lowers readout and processing overhead. Fabricated in an 180-nm CMOS process, the prototype operates at 30f/s with a total power consumption of 64.6mW and achieves an imaging figure of merit (FoM) of 2853.2pixel•f/s/mW, corresponding to a  $6\times $  promotion compared with conventional full-array 3-D detection at the same resolution. The proposed sensing–computation co-designed framework provides an energy-efficient and scalable solution for high-resolution flash LiDAR in power-constrained embedded platforms.},
