  abstract={This article presents an extendable wideband band-divided SP $n$ T switch topology based on a reconfigurable pole-to-throw network (RPTN). Compared to conventions, it mitigates the loss degradation resulting from complex signal routing in advanced RF front-ends. This is enabled by the RPTN, which divides the throws into low-/mid-/high-band (HB) groups and reshapes the pole-to-throw interaction in different operating modes. It allows off-state parasitics to be incorporated into mode-dependent matching networks or bypassed. To demonstrate the scalability and technology portability of the proposed topology, two wideband switch chips in SP3T and SP6T configurations are implemented in different processes. The SP3T prototype is fabricated in a 0.25- $\mu $ m GaN HEMT process with a modest figure of merit (FoM) of 500 fs. Measurements show an insertion loss (IL) of 0.42–1.47 dB, a return loss (RL) of better than 14.6 dB, and a P0.1dB of 35.2–40 dBm over a DC–18 GHz frequency range. The SP6T prototype is fabricated in a 0.15- $\mu $ m GaAs pHEMT process with a favorable FoM of 240 fs. Measurements demonstrate an IL of 0.34–1.18 dB, an RL of higher than 15.0dB, and a P0.1dB of 25.6–29.7 dBm over a DC–18 GHz. The measured results of both prototypes verify the effectiveness and scalability of the proposed topology.},
