  abstract={This article investigates dynamic leakage-suppression (DLS) logic for minimum-power operation in digital circuits. The DLS logic family uses a strong intrinsic subthreshold leakage reduction technique and is an emerging approach for computing applications that prioritize low active power at or beneath the nW-level. We analytically model the key operating characteristics of DLS logic and show that a minimum-power point exists at an optimal  $V_{\mathrm {dd}}$  that balances various sources of leakage current. We discuss available knobs for DLS logic and present a general design methodology for targeting minimum-power operation while also considering robustness. A fabricated test chip in 65-nm CMOS validates our models and design methodology with several unique DLS standard cell libraries, and measurements of traditional static-CMOS logic are included for comparison. Measurement results demonstrate a minimum DLS leakage power of 0.26 fW per inverter at 0.35 V, over  $350\times $  lower than CMOS inverters at the same supply voltage. At 1.2 V, this leakage reduction increases to  $\gt$   $ 1000\times $ . The library implementation of the proposed DLS libraries is validated with synthesized 8-bit DLS multiply-accumulate (MAC) circuits.},
