  abstract={Large-scale neural recording systems for in vitro bioelectronic interfaces require ultra-low noise, scalability to thousand-channel arrays, and long-term reliability to avoid chip replacement during biological experiments. This article presents a 1024-channel neural readout integrated circuit (IC) capable of both extracellular and intracellular recordings, based on a voltage-controlled oscillator (VCO)-based continuous-time delta–sigma modulator (CTDSM) architecture. A slot-level channel replacement scheme is incorporated to improve system-level robustness against degraded or faulty analog front-end (AFE) channels, with potential benefit for manufacturing tolerance and long-term operation in large-scale microelectrode array (MEA) systems. Each slot integrates 32 regular channels and four spare channels, enabling faulty or degraded channels to be detected and replaced at the slot level, thereby relaxing the stringent single-channel yield requirements of large-scale neural interfaces. Fabricated in a 28-nm CMOS FDSOI process, the proposed IC achieves input-referred noise (IRN) of  $8.06~\mu $ Vrms in the local field potential (LFP) band (0.5 Hz–1 kHz) and  $5.34~\mu $ Vrms in the action potential (AP) band (300 Hz–10 kHz). After channel replacement, the measured SNDR and THD are 56.36 dB and 0.074%, respectively, with a total chip power consumption of 82.4mW. In vitro experiments with cultured neurons validate stable multi-channel neural recording over several weeks.},
