  abstract={This article presents a pseudo-pseudo-differential (PPD) noise-shaping (NS) successive-approximation-register (SAR) analog-to-digital converter (ADC). A PPD-based split-capacitor stacking scheme is introduced to enable accurate passive summation. Compared to the conventional capacitor stacking scheme, the proposed scheme reduces the noise transfer function (NTF) sensitivity to parasitic capacitors. Combined with a third-order recycling integrator, the proposed architecture realizes a sharp and high-efficiency NTF. The dynamic-range (DR) degradation caused by mismatch-error shaping (MES) is mitigated through PPD-based prediction, even under input common-mode voltage deviation from the system common-mode level. Moreover, signal-to-noise-and-distortion ratio (SNDR) degradation for differential input amplitudes smaller than the predictor and amplifier offsets is mitigated by intentionally shifting the input common-mode voltage. Implemented in a 180-nm CMOS process, the prototype ADC achieves a measured SNDR of 93.5 dB over a 1-kHz bandwidth. Operating from a 1.2-V supply, it consumes  $3.7~\mu $ W, corresponding to an SNDR-based Schreier figure of merit (FoMSNDR) of 177.8 dB. The measured SNDR variation remains within 4.4 dB across an input common-mode voltage range of 0.2–1.0 V.},
