  abstract={Integrated DC–DC converters, for the sake of small form factor, typically employ small-valued passives to facilitate on-chip or in-package integration. The crucial attributes of these converters for next-generation subminiature Internet of Things include low cost, small form factor, and high power efficiency. Achieving all of these attributes remains a formidable challenge for state-of-the-art integrated converters. In this article, we present an integrated DC–DC converter that concurrently achieves these three attributes by means of two innovations. First, a proposed high-inductance-density bond-wire inductor featuring a novel coil pattern leads to  $\gt$   $ 110\times $  inductance-density improvement over contemporary bond-wire inductors. Second, a proposed low quiescent current pulse-frequency-modulation (PFM) controller embodies a proposed voltage-controlled-oscillator modulator and a proposed switched-capacitor zero-crossing detector. Owing to the novel modulator and zero-crossing detector, the controller features a low quiescent current of  $\sim$   $ 65~\mu $ A, without compromising its very-high-frequency operation at 50 MHz. As a proof of concept, our complete integrated DC–DC converter realized in a  $3\times 3$  mm2 package embodies the proposed inductor and the proposed controller. By leveraging the said two innovations, our integrated converter features low cost, subminiature form factor, and high light-load power efficiency. Compared to state-of-the-art integrated converters, our converter occupies a  $\sim$   $ 40\times $  smaller form factor yet features  $\ge$   $ 8$ % higher power efficiency at a light load of 50% voltage conversion ratio, from 10- to 50-mA load.},
  keywords={Inductors;Loading;Converters;DC-DC power converters;Wire;Wires;Wiring;Bonding;Current;Lighting;Bond-wire inductor;controller;DC–DC converter;Internet of Things (IoT);modulator;power management IC;pulse frequency modulation (PFM);zero-crossing detector},
