  abstract={This article presents an electrical bio-impedance (bioZ) spectroscopy integrated circuit (IC) that achieves both high-throughput and high-accuracy. The proposed phase-locked sampling (PLS) scheme, which employs a sampling phase-locked loop (SPLL) and a reference resistor ( $R_{\textit {REF}}$ ), enables fast and precise impedance demodulation. By extracting the impedance components through sampling rather than relying on DC settling, the proposed approach overcomes the inherent settling-time limitations of prior demodulation-based bioZ systems, thereby enabling high-throughput operation. In addition, the use of a reference resistor with the identical signal path of the target impedance allows effective cancellation of errors originating from the current generator (CG) as well as chip-to-chip variations in the amplifier and low-pass filter (LPF), significantly improving measurement accuracy. To further support wideband operation with low power consumption, the input signal is pre-demodulated to an intermediate frequency. This technique relaxes the operating frequency requirement of the readout IC, enabling a simple and efficient implementation of the sampling-based architecture. Measurement results demonstrate magnitude and phase errors of less than 1.78% and 1.8°, respectively, over a wide frequency range from 4 kHz to 2 MHz. Furthermore, the proposed IC achieves an input-referred impedance noise of 34.9 m $\Omega $ / $\sqrt {}$ Hz while supporting an output data rate (ODR) of 4–40 kS/s.},
