  pages={1-15},
  abstract={In this article, a four-element digital-modulated phased-array transmitter based on quadrature switched/floated-capacitor power amplifiers (SFCPAs) and reconfigurable switched-capacitor tuning lines (RSCTLs) is proposed. Phase shifting in each element is achieved by hybrid coarse and fine phase-tuning approaches. The SFCPAs with the quadrature-rotation technique are presented for coarse phase tuning. The baseband signals for the quadrature SFCPAs are preset based on the phase-shifting codes. Meanwhile, the RSCTLs are used for fine phase tuning. Capacitors with various capacitances are switched on under different phase-shifting states. To improve the efficiency at deep power back-off (PBO) peaks, hybrid Doherty and impedance boosting techniques are utilized. A 4-to-1 reconfigurable transformer is introduced in the SFCPAs for flexible impedance tuning. To improve isolation among four elements, active power dividers are utilized. The proposed phased-array transmitter is implemented and fabricated in conventional 40-nm-CMOS technology with a chip size of 8.32 mm2. The fabricated phased-array transmitter features a 28.4 dBm peak output power and 37.9% peak system efficiency. Meanwhile, 37.9%/33.7%/28.7%/23.5%/17.6%/12.9% system efficiency is achieved under 0/3/6/9/12/15-dB PBOs at 2.4 GHz. In addition, it supports 15-bit phase-tuning resolution with rms phase/power error of  $0.12\boldsymbol {^{\circ } }$ /0.15 dB. For 25 MHz 256-QAM/40 MHz 64-QAM modulation signals, it exhibits EVM of 2.22%/4.69%,  $\boldsymbol {P_{avg}}$  of 21.5 dBm/22.7 dBm, and average system efficiency of 23.2%/26.1% at 2.4 GHz. Utilizing a monopole antenna with a gain of 4.21 dBi, peak EIRP of 43.5 dBm is obtained. Meanwhile, the radiation patterns with  $0\boldsymbol {^{\circ } }$ ,  $15\boldsymbol {^{\circ } }$ ,  $30\boldsymbol {^{\circ } }$ , and  $45\boldsymbol {^{\circ } }$  beam steering are measured based on the monopole antenna array.},
