  pages={1-15},
  abstract={Qubit-based quantum annealing processors operate at ultra-low temperatures (15 mK) requiring enormous cooling energy. Consequently, there is a lot of interest in quantum Monte Carlo (QMC) algorithms that can be used to emulate quantum computing on classical machines. However, prior classical emulators implemented on CPUs, GPUs, or field-programmable gate arrays (FPGAs) are orders of magnitude slower than quantum annealers highlighting the need for dedicated application-specific integrated circuits (ASICs). Over the past decade, many ASICs have been developed, including general Ising solvers, typically implemented as digital system on chip (SoCs) targeting highly complex graphs, and specific Ising solvers, which address relatively simple Ising graphs using analog computing paradigms. General solvers often suffer from high power consumption, while specific solvers lack reconfigurability. This work presents, for the first time, a QMC algorithm implemented on a 3-D Ising computer fabricated in commercial 65-nm CMOS technology. Specifically, it solves a quantum problem in which a transverse field Ising (TFI) Hamiltonian is applied to an unconventional octagonal lattice. The reconfigurable integrated circuit (IC) also supports traditional 2-D Ising models for solving combinatorial optimization problems (COPs), offering better reconfigurability than prior specific Ising solvers. This IC achieved an energy-to-solution of  $41.9~\mu $ J at 1 MHz and  $16.65~\mu $ J at 10 MHz at 0.5 V for the QMC problem. Compared to previous FPGA implementations, this is a three-order-of-magnitude improvement and a six-order-of-magnitude improvement over CPU-based solutions. This article also proposes a new energy efficiency metric,  $E_{\text {ELOP}}$  (Energy per ELementary OPeration), to better benchmark p-bit-based Ising solvers. Our IC achieves an  $E_{\text {ELOP}}$  of 1350 fJ at 1 MHz and 535 fJ at 10 MHz (measured at 0.5 V) for solving the QMC problem, which are also equivalent to the energy efficiency of 0.74 and 1.87 TELOPS/W (ELementary OPeration per Second per Watt).},
