  year={2026},
  volume={},
  number={},
  pages={1-11},
  abstract={This article presents a W-band active bidirectional vector-sum phase shifter (Bi-VSPS) using a bidirectional phase-inverting variable-gain amplifier (Bi-PIVGA) with back-gate biasing in 28-nm fully depleted silicon-on-insulator (FD-SOI) CMOS. The Bi-PIVGA employs a neutralization technique that cancels the gate-to-drain capacitances of both active and inactive transistors in each signal direction. By using FD-SOI back-gate biasing, the Bi-PIVGA reduces gain-state-dependent intrinsic-capacitance variation, thereby maintaining neutralization accuracy and minimizing port-impedance variation over the gain-control range. This maintains I/Q accuracy and enables low rms phase error (rms P.E.) and rms gain error (rms G.E.) over a wide bandwidth in both transmit (TX) and receive (RX) modes. The Bi-PIVGA exhibits input and output return losses > 10 dB for all gain states above 71.5 GHz (TX) and above 71.9 GHz (RX). The measured peak gain is 4.7 dB at 93.5 GHz (TX) and 94.5 GHz (RX). Using this Bi-PIVGA, the Bi-VSPS achieves an rms P.E.  ${\lt }~2.0{^{\circ }}$  and a maximum rms G.E.  ${\lt }~0.34$  dB across 74.7–97.4 GHz [26.4% fractional bandwidth (FBW)] in both modes. At the maximum-gain state, the measured average gain is −4.3 dB at 92.1 GHz (TX) and 92.7 GHz (RX), with a core area of 0.21 mm2 and 80-mW DC power consumption.},
