  abstract={This article presents a fully integrated output-capacitor-less (OCL) multi-feedback-loop low-dropout regulator (LDO) with an in-built single bipolar junction transistor (BJT)-based voltage and current reference (VCR) for energy-harvesting Internet of Things (IoT) devices. The proposed architecture comprises four loops, which significantly enhance the DC regulation and transient performance of the LDO. A tri-loop regulation core based on a flipped-voltage-follower (FVF) structure offers high DC loop gain, resulting in enhanced line and load regulation. A replica-based slew-rate enhancement (RSRE) loop improves the undershoot and overshoot recovery times during load transient response. Furthermore, the proposed LDO consumes less area and power, and exhibits reduced output noise by integrating a single-BJT branch bandgap reference (BGR) with an FVF power stage, unlike conventional LDOs that use externally connected BGRs, which require multiple resistors and BJTs. The bias current is generated internally by a proposed current reference (CR) based on a shared single-BJT and is powered by the LDO output voltage to improve current line sensitivity (LS). The proposed design is fabricated in a 65-nm low-power (LP) CMOS technology and occupies an area of 0.041 mm2. The proposed LDO delivers a maximum load current of 50 mA at a nominal output voltage of 1.2 V. Measurement results show that the proposed LDO achieves line and load regulation of 0.66 mV/V and 0.195 mV/mA, respectively. The measured average temperature coefficients (TCs) of the output voltage and bias reference current are 6.9 and 86.55 ppm/°C, respectively, over  $- 40~^{\circ } $ C to  $85~^{\circ } $ C, with corresponding voltage and current accuracies ( $\sigma /\mu $ ) of 0.16% and 1.42%. The measured power supply rejection (PSR) at 10 Hz is 67.5 dB at maximum load current. The proposed LDO consumes a 268-nA quiescent current at room temperature with a minimum supply voltage of 1.3 V. We achieve a