  abstract={This article presents a Ka-band phased-array multiple-input multiple-output (MIMO) receiver for sixth-generation (6G) wireless networks. A time-division (TD) MIMO architecture is combined with DP antenna array to support eight MIMO streams in the Ka band. The complex weights of each RF element in both polarization arrays are sequentially switched at the Nyquist rate to enable fast-stream switching for MIMO stream coexistence. The TD approach achieves full phased-array reuse and delivers high array gain and spatial selectivity for each stream at the expense of generating switching harmonics. To address this issue, a non-uniform time-hopping (TH) sequence is introduced in this article to spectrally spread the switching harmonics and mitigate the direct blocker folding. A proof-of-concept phased-array receiver is fabricated in 65-nm CMOS, integrating eight RF elements in a 7.2-mm2 core area and consuming 335.2 mW. Combined with DP antennas, the phased-array receiver supports  $4 \times 4$  TD-MIMO across both polarizations and achieves a maximum data rate of 38.4Gb/s using eight 800-MHz 64-QAM signals in over-the-air (OTA) measurements. Benefiting from the TD-MIMO architecture, the phased-array receiver achieves 0.078 mm2 and 5.23 mW per element per stream. With the proposed non-uniform TH (NUTH) dithering, deep notches are generated at switching harmonics and 42.9-dB alias suppression is achieved. The proposed phased-array receiver also demonstrates a 34-dB improvement in blocker tolerance, maintaining –30-dB EVMrms under a 0-dBc in-band blocker sweeping across –90° to 90° field of view (FoV) in MIMO OTA measurements.},
