  pages={1-15},
  abstract={Combinatorial optimization problems (COPs) play a critical role in various domains but are computationally challenging. Although coherent Ising machines (CIMs) exploit quantum-inspired dynamics to solve COPs, their optical implementations suffer from bulky components and high system cost. In this work, we present the first fully-integrated CMOS-based simulated CIM (SCIM). The proposed design integrates a Booth-optimized multiplier–accumulator and piecewise linear activation circuit, thereby reducing power consumption and area. A reconfigurable fully-connected topology enables direct mapping of a wide range of COPs, supporting variable spin counts and coupling bit-widths. We further introduce a dynamic pump-rate control that accelerates escape from high-energy states and enables fine-grained search near the bifurcation point, along with a spin freezing (SF) technique that stabilizes converged spins and eliminates redundant updates. Fabricated in 28-nm CMOS technology, SCIM occupies 0.354 mm2 and consumes only 2.952 mW. For MAX-CUT benchmarks, it achieves time-to-solution (TTS) ranging from 0.15 to  $30~\mu $ s, corresponding to a  $2\times $ – $1653\times $  speedup over state-of-the-art hardware implementations in terms of normalized TTS. Furthermore, the number partitioning problem (NPP) and multiple-input multiple-output (MIMO) detection are performed using the proposed SCIM, which achieves high-performance solving, thereby demonstrating a reconfigurable, power-efficient, and high-speed approach for physics-inspired combinatorial optimization.},
