  pages={1-10},
  abstract={This work presents an 830.7-MHz on-off-keying (OOK) transmitter (TX) based on a MEMS oscillator employing pulse-driven and energy-recycling techniques. To improve system efficiency, stacked inverter-based power amplifier (PA) is utilized, while energy-recycling from the top stacked PA is exploited to power the oscillator. An on-chip 2.7:1 transformer is designed to boost the load impedance seen by the PA, thereby maximizing PA efficiency without the need for an external matching network. The proposed TX is fabricated in a TSMC 40-nm CMOS process and occupies a die area of 1.73mm2. The MEMS oscillator consumes only  $176.5~{\mu }$ W while achieving a phase noise of −118.2dBc/Hz at a 100-kHz offset. Under continuous operation, the TX supports a data rate of 100Mb/s, resulting in an energy efficiency of 2.88pJ/bit at an output power of −15dBm. By adopting a duty-cycling ratio of 4%, the TX consumes only  $13.8~{\mu }$ W at a data rate of 3Mb/s, which is at least  $7{\times }$  lower than that of other reported works.},
